+## 9. Greedy replay: off vs A (`--spec-type ngram-mod` defaults), 2026-10-12
+
+Both arms use the full production cmd on :8089 (port, `--mmproj`, q4_0 KV, `--n-gpu-layers 99`, `-np 2`, `--kv-unified`, `--jinja`, `LLAMA_ATTN_ROT_DISABLE=1`); a **fresh server per phase** (the n-gram index persists across requests, so a fresh start prevents inflated acceptance). **No `-nommproj` fallback fired** for either arm — speculation ran *with* mmproj on text-only traffic, confirming §3 and the standing facts. `draft_n`/`draft_n_accepted` are present (the server reports them only when `n_draft_tokens>0`, `tools/server/server-common.cpp:82-85`); they appear in the A rows and are 0 in off.
+
+Per-phase foreground decode tok/s = `sum(predicted_n)/sum(predicted_ms)` over the phase's `fg` requests:
+
+| phase | off | A (ngram-mod) | Δ |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| single-agent (fg agent) | 55.1 | 86.0 | **+56%** |
+| single-mem (fg mem) | 55.6 | 53.1 | −4.5% |
+| dual-agent (fg agent) | 26.3 | 25.5 | −3% |
+| dual-mem (fg mem) | 25.0 | 23.8 | −5% |
+
+Combined both-slots throughput over busy time (`sum` over all requests in the phase / `sum(predicted_ms)`): **dual-agent 25.6→26.1 tok/s (+2%, neutral)**; **dual-mem 26.1→25.0 tok/s (−4%)**.
+
+Draft acceptance (A): single-agent agent 75.5% (6212/8229), single-mem 9.3% (69/740), dual-agent fg agent 14.0% (259/1856). Length-finish rows: off 1 (single-agent), A 5.
+
+**Read.** A is a strong single-slot agent win (+56%) but is **neutral — and slightly worse — when both slots are busy**, which is exactly the case the deploy decision rule targets. Dual-phase fg tok/s is dragged by the other slot's large prompt prefills (dual-agent fg agent stalls behind ~36k-token mem prefills) and dual-agent fg acceptance is only 14.0%.
+
+**Caveat on the comparison:** with `-np 2` the batch mix changes the logits, so the *same request produces different greedy output text/length across arms*. Of the 99 `(phase,id)` rows shared between off and A, 65 differ in `out_sha256` (e.g. `single-mem` `inventory-mem0-r0/1/0`: off 283 vs A 307 tokens). Per-request tok/s is therefore compared across slightly different workloads; the **aggregate both-slots throughput is the fairer decision metric**. Output identity within a phase (off vs A) is not expected nor required; the decision rests on throughput.
+
+### B choice: `--spec-type ngram-cache`
+
+Chosen to be maximally informative for the dual-slot decision, given A is neutral there:
+
+- **It changes the mechanism, not just a parameter.** A is neutral in the dual case because *acceptance* is low on the background mem work (9.3% single, 12.7% dual-agent bg). A plain ngram-mod parameter tweak (e.g. larger `n-max`) would not raise acceptance for that shared-prompt JSON output and would mostly add wasted verify work; ngram-cache attacks the *index-rebuild cost* that dominates the repeated background requests.
+- **The dual phase is dominated by repeated mem requests.** The background loop replays the 12 mem requests (dual-mem) / up to 60–80 bg mem (dual-agent), all reusing the same ~33.6k-char `ADDITIVE_EXTRACTION_PROMPT`. Index build is O(prompt_n × n-max) hash inserts per request; `ngram-cache` persists the index, so repeated bg requests cost almost nothing, freeing GPU cycles for the foreground.
+- **It is a distinct n-gram family** (listed in the `--spec-type` help, §1), giving independent information for the deploy/no-deploy call.
+- **Safe on VRAM.** The n-gram index/checkpoints live in host RAM (engine-review.md: `--cache-ram`, 8 GiB default), not the GPU. Both off and A measured 9507/10240 MiB; ngram-cache adds host-RAM index, not GPU memory. If the server refused (e.g. an `unsupported by this context` line), the script auto-reruns as `B-nommproj` and logs the triggering line.
+
+Next: after B/greedy, run `off-prod` and `<max(A,B)>-prod` (prod sampling), then the decision page.
